 {:path "<kotoba-lang>/lang/selfhost-compiler-grant.edn",
  :text
  ";; The capability grant the Kotoba compiler (amu) holds when it runs as a\n;; program -- the abilities its own modules name (superproject\n;; adr-2609242330 S5). scripts/measure-selfhost-distance.cljk passes this as\n;; `--policy` on the project route, so a compiler module that names an\n;; ability is admitted with the authority the compiler actually has, and a\n;; refusal on the scoreboard is a language wall rather than \"no grant was\n;; given to a measurement\".\n;;\n;; Every entry is a catalog capability (lang/capability-catalog.edn) with a\n;; handler in the nbb mechanism layer (amu src/kotoba/compiler/nbb/host/).\n;; All but hash/sha256 also have a Linux static ELF handler (adr-2609251400\n;; C3). hash/sha256 is answered by the kexe loader (wire 3) and has no C3\n;; handler yet; the pure org-nist-sha2 computes the same value, and amu\n;; test/nbb/host_abilities.cljk pins that the nbb handler agrees with it.\n;; process/spawn and entropy/draw join when a compiler module names them.\n;;\n;; Eight entries, not nine: fs/browse (wire 34) is left out although both\n;; runtimes answer it, because no compiler module names it yet and a policy\n;; with nine or more grants is refused by amu (measured 2026-09-25 at amu\n;; 8260750a: nine grants answer `internal compiler error` from `check`,\n;; eight are admitted -- the same limit adr-2609251400 C1 records as\n;; \"malformed\" on the compile route). Add it when a module needs it and the\n;; limit is understood.\n{:allow #{[:cap/call :cli/args]\n          [:cap/call :clock/now]\n          [:cap/call :env/read]\n          [:cap/call :fs/app-data]\n          [:cap/call :hash/sha256]\n          [:cap/call :io/read]\n          [:cap/call :io/write]\n          [:cap/call :io/write-error]}}\n"},
